{
  "statement": "Election-cycle utility mandates for frontier AI labs are directionally correct but structurally incomplete. Without binding technical enforcement, strict liability at each node of the chain, and ecosystem-wide coverage that follows manipulation wherever it migrates, the mandate becomes a Potemkin regulation—protecting the appearance of democratic integrity while the substance erodes underneath. The question before policymakers is not whether to regulate AI during elections. It is whether the proposed regulation has an enforcement mechanism or is governance theater.\n\nThe enforcement gap is the central vulnerability. The C2PA content provenance standard—the leading technical framework for watermarking AI-generated content—exists today but remains voluntary. Its metadata can be stripped from an image in seconds using any basic editing tool. The EU AI Act, the most ambitious AI regulatory framework in the world, openly acknowledges enforcement gaps for cross-border AI services. We have seen this pattern before. The FAA delegated Boeing 737 MAX safety certification to Boeing itself. Three hundred forty-six people died because the enforcement mechanism was nominal, not operational. Election-cycle neutrality mandates face the same structural failure: the rule exists on paper while the technical capacity to verify compliance does not exist in practice. Provenance mandates must be paired with strict product liability for labs that release systems without tamper-resistant provenance. Shift the burden from detection to prevention. Fund independent audit bodies with rotating personnel and adversarial red-team mandates to resist the regulatory capture that destroyed FAA oversight of Boeing.\n\nAccountability fragments across labs, platforms, regulators, and civil society so thoroughly that when AI-generated electoral manipulation succeeds, it becomes everyone's problem and no one's fault. Section 230 shields platforms from liability for AI-generated content distributed by users. No settled product liability doctrine covers AI-generated election content. The four-actor responsibility model this debate proposes—regulators, labs, platforms, civil society—replicates exactly the diffuse causal chain that produces accountability voids. The NTSB model for aviation accident investigation works because it assigns investigative authority to a single independent body with subpoena power. Election-integrity enforcement needs the same: a designated federal authority with binding power to halt noncompliant AI deployments during election windows, not a distributed hope that four actors will coordinate under pressure.\n\nRegulating only frontier labs while ignoring the broader ecosystem creates a regulatory monoculture that displaces manipulation to open-weight models and unregulated platforms. Llama 3, Mistral, and Stable Diffusion weights are already distributed globally and cannot be recalled. The waterbed effect is documented: when Twitter and Facebook deplatformed coordinated inauthentic networks, those networks migrated to Telegram and smaller platforms during elections in Brazil, India, and the Philippines. A mandate covering only OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google regulates the most visible surface while leaving the actual attack vectors untouched. This is not an argument against regulation. Fire codes did not become futile because arsonists exist. It is an argument for ecosystem-wide governance—provenance requirements at the distribution layer, not just the generation layer—so that platforms carrying AI-generated election content bear independent obligations regardless of where the content originated.\n\nElections are time-bounded, irreversible events where the correction window closes permanently on election day. Deepfake audio of Slovak Prime Minister Pellegrini circulated forty-eight hours before the September 2023 election, inside the media quiet period when corrections could not propagate. No documented case exists where post-election fact-checking reversed the electoral effect of pre-election synthetic content. The asymmetry is categorical: a temporarily over-restricted AI output during a sixty-day election window is a recoverable cost; a permanently distorted election outcome is not. My assumptions are transparent. First, independent audit bodies can be constituted with structural safeguards against capture—rotating auditors, public funding, adversarial mandates. Second, provenance technology can be made tamper-resistant enough to impose meaningful friction, even if not perfect. Those assumptions are testable and falsifiable. The alternative—deploying frontier AI into election cycles with no enforceable provenance, no assigned liability, and no rollback authority—is not testable. It is a bet we place once, on an outcome we cannot reverse.",
