es hand political speech gatekeeping power to whoever writes the definition of \"neutral.\" The FCC's Fairness Doctrine ran from 1949 to 1987 and was abandoned because fairness proved impossible to adjudicate without embedding the political preferences of the adjudicators. No jurisdiction on earth has successfully defined and enforced AI content neutrality at any scale. The sixty-day election window creates maximum political stakes for whoever controls that definition. The populations most likely to have their speech flagged as non-neutral or lacking proper provenance credentials are grassroots campaigns, minority political movements, and independent media outlets—exactly the actors with the least institutional capacity to contest false positives in real time. The Safetyist frames a temporarily over-restricted AI output as a \"recoverable cost.\" Recoverable for whom? A major party candidate's campaign has lawyers and lobbyists to challenge a false flag within hours. A community organizer in Detroit does not. The costs of over-restriction fall on those with the least power to push back, and that distribution is not an accident—it is the predictable outcome of handing gatekeeping authority to institutions dominated by incumbent interests.\n\nThe entire debate frame needs to break open. This is not an AI generation problem. It is a democratic infrastructure problem being misdiagnosed as a technical one. Platform business models that maximize engagement by amplifying emotionally provocative content—including synthetic content—are the primary distribution vector for electoral manipulation, documented across elections in Brazil, India, and the Philippines. GDPR cookie consent showed what happens when you regulate the distribution layer without changing the underlying business model: you get performative compliance banners and no structural change. Regulating the supply side of synthetic content while leaving demand-side incentives—platform amplification algorithms optimized for ad revenue—completely intact is treating the symptom while protecting the disease vector. My argument depends on two assumptions I want to name openly: first, that platform amplification is a more significant vector for electoral manipulation than AI generation tools alone; second, that regulatory capture in this domain is near-certain given the information asymmetry between AI developers and any plausible regulatory body. If either assumption is wrong, the utility mandate looks more defensible. But here is the question neither of my opponents can answer comfortably: if the three regulated frontier labs comply perfectly with every provenance and neutrality mandate, and a synthetic deepfake generated by an open-weight model still swings a Senate race after being amplified by a platform's recommendation algorithm—who is accountable, and what was the regulation for?",
