cement point creates a single point of failure.\n\nTwo concessions sharpen the proposal. First, my earlier framing of Section 230 overstated the case. The legal status of AI-generated content under Section 230 is genuinely unresolved—courts have not decided whether AI output is third-party speech or platform-created content. That ambiguity is itself the problem. During a sixty-day election window, no platform general counsel can tell their engineers what obligations apply. Legislative clarification—not judicial improvisation—is what the architecture requires. Second, neutrality mandates as originally described are politically contestable and likely unenforceable. Strip them. Replace them with provenance-plus-disclosure obligations: labs must watermark at generation, platforms must surface provenance metadata to users at distribution, and the NTSB-model authority audits compliance with these technically specifiable requirements. This is not adjudicating \"neutrality.\" It is requiring machine-readable labels on synthetic content, the same way the FDA requires ingredient labels on food.\n\nThe Accelerationist frames suppressed speech during a sixty-day window as an irreversible cost symmetric with a manipulated election. These are not symmetric. Speech delayed by a compliance review can be published after review. Legal challenges can restore it. The speaker retains the underlying right. A stolen election outcome cannot be reversed: the winner takes office, judicial appointments proceed, policies bind millions. Pre-election advertising blackout periods already exist in France, Canada, and India. The European Court of Human Rights has upheld them as proportionate restrictions on expression to protect electoral integrity. The asymmetry between a reversible false positive—delayed speech—and an irreversible false negative—a manipulated election outcome—is what justifies precautionary architecture during bounded windows. This is not a novel principle. It is the logic democracies already apply to broadcast media.\n\nThe Skeptic's accountability-void scenario—three compliant frontier labs, an open-weight deepfake from an unregulated model swings a Senate race—has a specific answer under layered liability. Labs that release open-weight models without tamper-resistant provenance bear product liability for foreseeable misuse, the same way a gun manufacturer that removes serial numbers bears liability for untraceable weapons. Platforms that amplify synthetic content without surfacing available provenance metadata bear independent liability for the amplification decision. The NTSB-model authority has jurisdiction over both layers, with subpoena power to trace the supply chain post-incident. Two assumptions undergird this architecture: first, that cryptographic watermarking can be made robust enough to survive common transformations like screenshotting and compression at rates above current C2PA baselines; second, that a single federal authority can maintain independence from the labs it regulates during election windows without the regulatory capture the Skeptic predicts. If either assumption fails, the architecture weakens. But the alternative—distribution-layer-only enforcement that the Skeptic's own evidence suggests will become performative—is not a safer bet. It is a bet that a single point of failure will hold when every precedent in product safety law says it will not.",
