  "statement": "Dropping neutrality mandates in favor of provenance-plus-disclosure is genuine progress, and I won't pretend otherwise. That concession moves the proposal closer to what procedural, checkable verification should look like — labels on outputs, not judgments about truth. But the implementation details as described would still consolidate AI capability in a handful of frontier labs and destroy the open-weight ecosystem that is the single best check against oligarchic control of this technology. The question is no longer whether provenance is good — it is — but who pays for it, who enforces it, and whether open-weight model releases become strict liability events. If the answer to that last question is yes, then three to five labs with billion-dollar legal departments become the only entities that can ship AI during election season. That is not safety. That is incumbent protection wearing safety's uniform. The AI industry is a projected $244 billion market according to the AI Civil Rights Act introduced by Rep. Clarke and Sen. Markey — and the compliance architecture we choose now will determine whether that value concentrates or distributes.\n\nThe pharmaceutical analogy breaks at its load-bearing joint. Pfizer manufactures a rivalrous physical product with a traceable chain of custody from factory to pharmacy to patient. When a batch is contaminated, Pfizer can recall it. AI model weights are non-rivalrous digital artifacts. Meta cannot recall Llama weights from 100,000 servers any more than Merriam-Webster can recall dictionaries from 100,000 bookshelves. The gun-serial-number comparison is worse: it implies that releasing open-weight models is manufacturing untraceable weapons. Follow that logic and you make the entire open-source software tradition — Linux, OpenSSL, encryption libraries — legally suspect, because every one of those tools has been used for foreseeable misuse. The correct analogy is publishing, not pharmaceuticals. We do not hold dictionary publishers liable when someone writes a threatening letter. We hold the person who wrote it accountable, and we hold the platform that amplified it accountable. AI accountability has a genuine bootstrap problem — we lack the mature liability infrastructure that pharmaceuticals built over decades — and the pharmaceutical analogy papers over that gap by pretending the infrastructure already exists. Joint-and-several liability for digital artifacts that cannot be recalled or traced post-release is not a regulatory framework. It is a legal fiction that would chill every open-weight release overnight.\n\nThe false-positive/false-negative asymmetry is real in the abstract. A stolen election is worse than delayed speech — I will not pretend otherwise. But the asymmetry collapses when you look at what France, Canada, and India actually regulate. France's 44-hour blackout applies to paid political advertising by identified candidates and parties. Canada's Election Act restricts \"election advertising\" as legally defined — paid messaging by registered entities. India's Model Code of Conduct governs political parties and candidates, not the general public. None of these precedents regulate the generation layer of a general-purpose technology. Applying blackout logic to all AI-generated content during a 60-day window is like banning all printing presses during election season because campaign flyers exist. The scope difference is not incremental — it is categorical. And here is where the asymmetry actually inverts in practice: speech \"delayed\" past election day by a compliance review is not delayed. It is suppressed. A grassroots organizer whose AI-generated voter-turnout video gets flagged on October 30 and cleared on November 6 did not experience a recoverable false positive. They experienced permanent silencing on the only question that mattered. The people who can absorb that delay are the people with legal teams — which means the asymmetry argument, applied at this scope, systematically advantages institutional speakers over grassroots ones.\n\nBoth of the named assumptions deserve scrutiny with current deployment data. On watermark robustness: common image transformations — JPEG compression, cropping, screenshot-and-reupload — strip C2PA metadata at rates above 90% in real-world conditions. The proposal's entire enforcement mechanism depends on watermarks surviving transformations they currently do not survive. On regulator independence: the 60-day election window creates maximum capture incentive because the stakes are highest and the timeline is shortest. The FEC has deadlocked on enforcement actions in every contested election cycle since 2010. The FCC's behavior during election seasons — selectively enforcing equal-time provisions — is a textbook case of capture under time pressure. As \"The rise of authoritarian informationalism\" documents, digitalization enables regimes to exercise sophisticated content control through mechanisms like keyword blocking. A 60-day compliance window with ambiguous enforcement authority is exactly the tool an authoritarian-leaning administration would need to selectively suppress opposition speech while claiming procedural neutrality.\n\nHere is the counter-proposal that preserves provenance without killing open-weight distribution. First, publicly fund provenance infrastructure — C2PA tooling as a shared utility that any model, open or closed, can plug into at near-zero marginal cost. Audit requirements that fall disproportionately on small labs unless publicly funded are not safety requirements — they are barriers to entry. Second, concentrate liability at the distribution and amplification layer, where electoral harm actually materializes — platforms that algorithmically amplify unlabeled AI content bear primary responsibility, not the model developer who released weights six months earlier. Third, replace pre-election compliance gatekeeping with a 90-day post-election audit mechanism — an NTSB-style investigation that identifies what went wrong and updates the rules for the next cycle, rather than a pre-election review process that suppresses speech in real time. This is a bounded, reversible experiment. The alternative — treating open-weight releases as strict liability events — is irreversible concentration of AI capability in the hands of incumbents. Who bears that cost? Every researcher, every nonprofit, every grassroots organization that cannot afford a billion-dollar legal department.",
